{
  "dom_id": "step-q73779",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\"",
